Subject: bring the toolbar to the reference's height

33 DIP, down from Chromium's 46 and from the 39 this project was stuck at.

The 39 held because two floors happened to be the same number. The top container
is sized as max(caption-button height, its own minimum) when the window has a
leading exclusion; on macOS that exclusion is 38, and the toolbar's own preferred
height was also 38. Every experiment so far had changed one side, so every
experiment measured 39, and the earlier conclusions -- first "the icon floors
it", then "the exclusion floors it" -- were each half right and therefore wrong.

A probe printing preferred_h, minimum_h and lead_excl_h from a running browser
settled it in one run: all three were 38.

So both sides move together. With a vertical tab strip the traffic lights sit
over the tab strip, which already leaves room for them, so the top container
skips the exclusion; and the toolbar's own content comes down through the
parameters already there -- an 18 DIP icon, 4 DIP button padding, a 25 DIP
location bar and a 3 DIP margin.

Verified: 33 DIP measured, traffic lights still fully visible and unoverlapped,
the URL still legible.

Why: the toolbar was the last measured difference from the reference.
Removable when: the toolbar's metrics are themable and the exclusion is not
applied to a column the caption buttons are not in.
